<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Betwa River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Betwa is a river of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> that rises in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vindhya-range">Vindhya Range</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> and flows north-east for about 590 km to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>. It runs through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundelkhand">Bundelkhand</a> region, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundela">Bundela</a> capital of Orchha was built on an island in its course in 1531.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/betwa-river.jpg" alt="Betwa River" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sharvarism · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beautiful_sunset_at_the_Royal_Chhatris_of_Orchha.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Betwa rises in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vindhya-range">Vindhya Range</a>, near Raisen, just north of Hoshangabad, now Narmadapuram, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>. It flows northeast through Madhya Pradesh, enters <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a> after flowing through Orchha, and meets the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> in Hamirpur district, where the Yamuna flows northward and the Betwa approaches from the south. Nearly half of its course runs over the Malwa Plateau, and the river is not navigable. Of its length of 590 kilometres, 232 kilometres lie in Madhya Pradesh and 358 kilometres in Uttar Pradesh. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> name is Vetravati.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> mentions the river as Vetravati, together with the Charmanwati, both tributaries of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a>. It was also known as Shuktimati, and the capital of the Chedi Kingdom stood on its banks. By an interstate agreement of 1973 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>, the Betwa River Board was constituted under the Betwa River Board Act of 1976, with ministers of the Union and of both states as members. Dams on the river include Rajghat, Matatila, Parichha, Dhurwara, Halali and Sukma Dukma. The Indian Navy named a frigate, INS Betwa, after the river.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Ken-Betwa River Linking Project was planned to transfer surplus water from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ken-river">Ken River</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> to the Betwa, through the Daudhan Dam and a canal of 2.5 km. The government said it would irrigate 1,060,000 hectares in drought-stricken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundelkhand">Bundelkhand</a>, supply drinking water to 6.2 million people and generate 130 MW of power, with completion expected in 2030 and a cost estimated at ₹440 billion.</p><p>Environmentalists warned that it would submerge nearly 98 sq km of the 543 sq km Panna Tiger Reserve. A 2019 expert panel appointed by the Supreme Court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> questioned its feasibility. Officials said all environmental clearances had been received. Ten villages were to be submerged and eleven more displaced, affecting over 7,000 families, mainly Gond and Kol, and residents protested.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Betwa River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kurwai was founded in 1713 by Muhammad Diler Khan, an Afghan officer in Mughal service, who received the territory near the <b>Betwa</b> river.</blockquote><a href="/state/kurwai-state">Kurwai State</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1531 the Bundela chief Rudra Pratap Singh founded Orchha on an island in the <b>Betwa</b> river and made it his capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/orchha-state">Orchha State</a></li></ul></section>
